            "text": "{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"wrong_strength\",\n      \"location\": \"(∃ L : ℝ, Tendsto (S x) atTop (𝓝 L))\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The informal problem asks to prove that the sequence converges. In the Lean proposition, convergence to some real number is an antecedent, not a conclusion.\",\n      \"prediction\": \"A proof of the proposition could assume this convergence rather than establish it.\",\n      \"probe_kind\": \"statement\",\n      \"comparison\": null,\n      \"bridge\": \"parse\",\n      \"obligation\": \"Confirm the implication's scope in Lean and whether the intended theorem requires convergence as a conclusion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"extra_hyp\",\n      \"location\": \"(∀ x ∈ T, Tendsto (S x) atTop (𝓝 (g x)))\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Continuity is conditional on the sequence already converging to g at every point of T. The informal problem asks for such a limit function and its continuity, without supplying global convergence to g as an assumption.\",\n      \"prediction\": \"A proof could use the assumed convergence to identify g with the sequence limits, without constructing g or proving that it is a limit function.\",\n      \"probe_kind\": \"statement\",\n      \"comparison\": null,\n      \"bridge\": \"parse\",\n      \"obligation\": \"Check the parsed scope of this antecedent and whether the informal wording intends g to be constructed rather than assumed to have the limit property.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The formal proposition makes both convergence and g's limit property assumptions in a conditional continuity claim, whereas the informal problem asks to establish them.\"\n}"
